INSERT INTO academy_daily_challenges (challenge_date, question, options, correct_index, explanation, xp_reward) VALUES
  ('2026-07-13', 'When you buy a share of stock, what do you actually own?', '["A loan you gave the company that they must pay back","A tiny piece of ownership in the company","A guarantee that your money will grow every year","A discount on the company''s products"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A share of stock represents a small slice of ownership in a company, so as a shareholder you own a piece of that business and can benefit if it grows in value.', 15),
  ('2026-07-14', 'What''s the main difference between owning a stock and owning a bond?', '["A stock means you own a piece of a company; a bond means you loaned money to be paid back","A stock is safer than a bond in every situation","A bond means you own part of a company; a stock is just a loan","There is no real difference between them"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A stock makes you a part-owner of a company, so you share in its ups and downs. A bond is more like an IOU—you lend money and expect it back with interest.', 15),
  ('2026-07-15', 'What is the main job of a stock exchange?', '["It sets the price you must pay for every stock","It connects people who want to buy with people who want to sell, and matches their orders","It lends money to companies that need cash","It decides which companies are good investments"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A stock exchange is like a giant marketplace: it matches a buyer''s order with a seller''s order when their prices line up, so a trade can happen. It doesn''t set prices itself — buyers and sellers do that through what they''re willing to accept.', 15),
  ('2026-07-16', 'You look up a stock and see: Last price $50, Change -$2.50 (-5%). What does this tell you?', '["The stock now costs $50 and has fallen $2.50 today, which is a 5% drop","The stock costs $2.50 and is up 5%","The stock will drop another $2.50 tomorrow","The company lost $50 in value overall"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ''last price'' is the most recent price a share traded at ($50), and the ''change'' shows it''s down $2.50 today — which equals a 5% drop from where it started the day.', 15),
  ('2026-07-17', 'What does a company''s ''market cap'' tell you, and how is it calculated?', '["The company''s total profit for the year","The share price multiplied by the total number of shares","The amount of cash the company keeps in the bank","The price of a single share of the company"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Market cap (short for market capitalization) is the total value of a company as seen by the stock market, found by multiplying the share price by the number of shares that exist. So a $50 stock with 1 million shares has a market cap of $50 million.', 15),
  ('2026-07-18', 'What do a stock''s ''52-week high'' and ''52-week low'' tell you?', '["The highest and lowest prices the stock traded at over the past year","How much profit the company made last year","The stock price for the next 52 weeks","How many shares were bought in a year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 52-week high and low simply show the top and bottom prices a stock reached during the past year, giving you a quick sense of its recent trading range.', 15),
  ('2026-07-19', 'When people talk about a stock''s ''trading volume,'' what do they mean?', '["The price of one share of the stock","The number of shares bought and sold during a period","The total profit the company made that year","The number of people who own the stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Trading volume counts how many shares changed hands over a set time, like a day. A sudden ''volume spike'' — way more trading than usual — often signals big news or strong interest, meaning many investors are reacting at once.', 15),
  ('2026-07-20', 'When you want to buy a stock right away, which price do you usually pay?', '["The bid price (what buyers are offering)","The ask price (what sellers are asking)","The average of yesterday''s prices","A price you get to choose freely"]'::jsonb, 1, 'To buy immediately, you pay the ''ask''—the lowest price a seller is willing to accept. The gap between the ask and the lower ''bid'' price is called the spread, a small cost of trading.', 15),
  ('2026-07-21', 'If lots of people suddenly want to buy a company''s stock but very few want to sell it, what usually happens to the stock''s price?', '["It goes up","It goes down","It stays exactly the same"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When demand (buyers) is greater than supply (sellers), the price rises because buyers compete and are willing to pay more to get the shares.', 15),
  ('2026-07-22', 'A company reports huge profits, but its stock barely moves. What does it mean when people say the good news was already "priced in"?', '["The company set the wrong price for its products","Investors already expected the good news, so it was reflected in the stock price beforehand","The stock is too expensive to buy right now","The news was fake and traders ignored it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When investors expect good news, they buy the stock ahead of time, pushing the price up before the announcement. So when the news actually arrives, it''s not a surprise and the price stays flat.', 15),
  ('2026-07-23', 'On a daily candlestick chart, what does the ''close'' price tell you?', '["The lowest price the stock hit that day","The price the stock was trading at when the market closed","The price the stock started at when the market opened","The average price for the whole day"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ''close'' is the final price when trading ends for the day. A candlestick shows four key prices: open (start), high (peak), low (bottom), and close (end).', 15),
  ('2026-07-24', 'You look at a stock''s 1-day chart and see it dropped sharply. Then you check the 1-year chart and see it''s actually way up over the year. Why can these two charts tell such different stories?', '["The 1-day chart is broken and showing wrong numbers","A 1-day chart shows a tiny recent slice, while a 1-year chart shows the bigger long-term trend","The 1-year chart always shows fake, made-up prices","A stock can only go one direction, so one chart must be lying"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A 1-day chart zooms into just today''s ups and downs, while a 1-year chart shows the longer journey — so a small dip today can still be a rise over the whole year.', 15),
  ('2026-07-25', 'What does a simple moving average (SMA) show on a stock chart?', '["The average price of a stock over a set period of time","The exact price the stock will reach tomorrow","The total number of shares a company has issued","The company''s profit for the year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An SMA adds up a stock''s prices over a chosen period (like 50 days) and divides by that number, creating a smooth line that helps you see the overall price trend without the daily ups and downs.', 15),
  ('2026-07-26', 'Why does spreading your money across many different investments help lower your risk?', '["Because owning more investments always guarantees you''ll make money","Because if one investment does poorly, others may do well and cushion the loss","Because it lets you avoid paying any taxes on your gains","Because the government protects investments that are spread out"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Diversification means not putting all your eggs in one basket. When your money is spread across many investments, a drop in one is often offset by others holding steady or rising, which smooths out the ups and downs.', 15),
  ('2026-07-27', 'What is one key difference between buying a single stock and buying an ETF?', '["A single stock is always cheaper than an ETF","An ETF holds many companies at once, while a single stock is just one company","A single stock never loses value, but an ETF can","An ETF can only be bought once per year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An ETF (exchange-traded fund) bundles many stocks together, so your money is spread across lots of companies, while a single stock ties your money to just one company''s fate.', 15),
  ('2026-07-28', 'What does "dollar-cost averaging" mean?', '["Investing a fixed amount of money at regular times, no matter the price","Only buying when prices are at their lowest","Spending the same amount on groceries each month","Selling all your investments once a year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dollar-cost averaging means putting in a set amount (like $100 every month) on a schedule, so you buy more shares when prices are low and fewer when high—which smooths out the ups and downs over time.', 15),
  ('2026-07-29', 'When you buy one share of a company''s stock, what do you actually own?', '["A tiny piece of the company itself","A loan the company must pay back to you","A guaranteed monthly paycheck from the company","A product the company makes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A share of stock represents a small ownership stake in a company, so when you own shares you''re a part-owner and can benefit if the company grows in value.', 15),
  ('2026-07-30', 'What is the main difference between owning a stock and owning a bond?', '["A stock means you own a piece of a company, while a bond means you lent money to a company or government","A stock is a loan, while a bond gives you company ownership","They are exactly the same thing with different names","A stock is only for governments, while a bond is only for companies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When you buy a stock, you become a part-owner of the company and share in its ups and downs. When you buy a bond, you''re acting like a lender, and the borrower promises to pay you back with interest.', 15),
  ('2026-07-31', 'When you buy a stock, what does a stock exchange actually do?', '["It matches your buy order with someone else''s sell order","It prints new money to pay for your shares","It decides on its own what price your stock should be"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A stock exchange is like a giant marketplace: it connects buyers and sellers, matching your buy order with someone willing to sell at that price so the trade can happen.', 15),
  ('2026-08-01', 'You look up a stock and see: Last Price $50, Change -$2, Change % -3.85%. What does this tell you?', '["The stock now costs $50 and is down $2 from yesterday''s close","The stock costs $2 and dropped $50","The stock went up by 3.85% today","The stock will cost $50 tomorrow"]'::jsonb, 0, '''Last Price'' is the most recent trading price ($50), and ''Change'' shows it''s $2 lower than the previous day''s closing price — the minus sign means it fell.', 15),
  ('2026-08-02', 'What does a company''s ''market cap'' tell you, and how is it figured out?', '["The total cash a company has in the bank","The total value of all its shares, found by multiplying share price by number of shares","How much profit the company made last year","The price of a single share of the company"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Market cap (short for market capitalization) is the company''s total value in the stock market. You calculate it by multiplying the share price by the total number of shares, so a $50 stock with 1 million shares has a $50 million market cap.', 15),
  ('2026-08-03', 'When you look up a stock, you see its ''52-week high'' and ''52-week low.'' What do these two numbers tell you?', '["The highest and lowest prices the stock traded at over the past year","How much profit the company made last year","The most and least the stock will ever be worth","The number of shares bought and sold today"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 52-week high and low simply show the top and bottom prices a stock hit during the past year, giving you a quick sense of its recent trading range — but they don''t predict where the price will go next.', 15),
  ('2026-08-04', 'You notice a stock that usually trades a few thousand shares a day suddenly trades millions of shares in one day. What does this ''volume spike'' usually signal?', '["The stock is about to be deleted from the market","Something important is happening, drawing lots of attention","The stock price can no longer change","Nothing at all — volume never means anything"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume is simply how many shares are being bought and sold. A sudden spike means many more people are trading than usual, which often points to big news or strong interest in that stock.', 15),
  ('2026-08-05', 'When you buy a stock, the price you usually pay is the ''ask'' price, and when you sell, you get the ''bid'' price. What is the small gap between these two prices called?', '["The commission","The spread","The dividend","The interest"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ''spread'' is the difference between the bid (sell) and ask (buy) prices. It''s a hidden cost of trading, because you buy a bit higher and sell a bit lower than the stock''s midpoint price.', 15),
  ('2026-08-06', 'Why does a company''s stock price usually go up on a given day?', '["The company printed more shares that morning","More people want to buy the stock than sell it","The stock market told the company to raise its price","The company deposited money into shareholders'' accounts"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stock prices move based on supply and demand: when more people want to buy than sell, the price rises, and when more want to sell than buy, it falls. No one ''sets'' the price directly.', 15),
  ('2026-08-07', 'A company reports strong profits that everyone was already expecting, but its stock barely moves. Why might that happen?', '["The good news was already ''priced in''","The company made a mistake in its report","Investors didn''t hear the news yet","Strong profits always lower a stock price"]'::jsonb, 0, '''Priced in'' means investors already expected the news and had bought the stock ahead of time, so the actual announcement brings no surprise and little price change.', 15),
  ('2026-08-08', 'On a daily candlestick chart, what does the ''close'' represent?', '["The highest price the stock reached that day","The price of the stock when the trading day ended","The price of the stock when the trading day began","The lowest price the stock reached that day"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ''close'' is the final price at the end of the trading day. A candlestick shows four key prices: open (start), high (peak), low (bottom), and close (end).', 15),
  ('2026-08-09', 'You check a stock and its 1-day chart looks scary and jagged, but its 1-year chart shows a smooth climb upward. What does this tell you?', '["The stock is broken because the two charts disagree","Short time frames show daily jitters, while longer ones show the bigger trend","The 1-day chart is always more accurate than the 1-year chart","You should sell immediately because of the jagged 1-day chart"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A 1-day chart zooms in on tiny, minute-by-minute wiggles, so it looks bumpy, while a 1-year chart zooms out to reveal the overall direction — both are true, just different views of the same stock.', 15),
  ('2026-08-10', 'On a stock chart, what does a simple moving average (SMA) show?', '["The average price over a set number of recent days, plotted as a smooth line","The exact price the stock will reach next week","The total number of shares a company has issued","The company''s yearly profit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An SMA adds up the closing prices over a chosen period (like 50 days) and divides by that number, creating a smooth line that helps you see the overall price trend without the daily ups and downs.', 15),
  ('2026-08-11', 'Why does spreading your money across many different investments lower your risk?', '["Because owning more investments guarantees you''ll make money","Because if one investment does badly, others may do well and balance it out","Because it lets you avoid paying any taxes","Because more investments always grow faster"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Diversification means not putting all your eggs in one basket. When your money is spread out, a loss in one investment can be softened by gains in others, so no single bad event wrecks your whole portfolio.', 15),
  ('2026-08-12', 'What''s the main difference between buying a single stock and buying an ETF?', '["A single stock is one company, while an ETF bundles many companies together","A single stock is safer than an ETF because it costs less","An ETF only holds cash, while a stock holds companies","There is no difference; they are just two names for the same thing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A single stock is a slice of just one company, so your money rises or falls with that one business. An ETF (exchange-traded fund) is a basket holding many companies at once, which spreads out your risk.', 15),
  ('2026-08-13', 'What does "dollar-cost averaging" mean when investing?', '["Investing a fixed amount of money at regular times, no matter the price","Waiting for prices to drop before investing all your money at once","Only buying stocks that cost less than one dollar","Selling a little bit of your investments every month"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dollar-cost averaging means putting in the same amount (like $100 every month) on a schedule, so you buy more shares when prices are low and fewer when high—which smooths out the ups and downs over time.', 15),
  ('2026-08-14', 'When you buy one share of a company''s stock, what do you actually own?', '["A loan you made to the company that they must pay back","A tiny piece of ownership in the company","A guarantee that you''ll get your money back","A discount card for the company''s products"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A share represents a small slice of ownership in a company, so as a shareholder you own a tiny part of that business and can benefit if it grows in value.', 15),
  ('2026-08-15', 'At a basic level, what''s the main difference between owning a stock and owning a bond?', '["A stock means you own a tiny piece of a company; a bond means you loaned money to be paid back with interest","A stock is always safer than a bond","A bond means you own part of a company; a stock is a loan you gave","There is no real difference between them"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A stock makes you a part-owner of a company, so you share in its ups and downs. A bond is more like an IOU: you lend money and expect it back later plus interest.', 15),
  ('2026-08-16', 'When you buy a stock, what does a stock exchange actually do?', '["It gives you a loan to buy the stock","It matches your buy order with someone else''s sell order","It decides how much a company is worth","It guarantees your stock will go up in value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A stock exchange is like a giant marketplace: it matches buyers with sellers so a trade can happen when both agree on a price.', 15),
  ('2026-08-17', 'You look up a stock and see: Last price $50, Change -$2, % Change -4%. What does this tell you?', '["The stock costs $50 and is up $2 today","The stock costs $50 and has dropped $2, which is 4% lower than yesterday","The stock will drop another 4% tomorrow","The stock costs $2 and changed by $50"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The last price ($50) is what the stock most recently traded for, the change (-$2) shows it fell $2 from the previous day''s close, and the % change (-4%) expresses that same drop as a percentage.', 15),
  ('2026-08-18', 'What does a company''s ''market cap'' tell you, and how is it calculated?', '["The total value of the company, found by multiplying its share price by the number of shares","The amount of cash the company has in the bank","The price of a single share of the company''s stock"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Market cap (short for market capitalization) is the company''s total value in the eyes of investors. You calculate it by multiplying the current share price by the total number of shares, so a $50 stock with 1 million shares has a $50 million market cap.', 15),
  ('2026-08-19', 'When you look up a stock, you see its "52-week high" and "52-week low." What do these two numbers tell you?', '["The highest and lowest prices the stock traded at over the past year","How much profit the company made last year","The price you''re guaranteed to pay if you buy today","The number of shares the company has sold this year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 52-week high and low simply show the top and bottom prices a stock reached during the past year, giving you a quick sense of its recent trading range and how today''s price compares.', 15),
  ('2026-08-20', 'When people talk about a stock''s ''trading volume,'' what do they mean?', '["The price the stock is trading at right now","The number of shares bought and sold during a period","The total profit the company made this year","The number of different investors who own the stock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume is simply how many shares changed hands in a given time, like a day. A sudden ''volume spike'' (way more shares traded than usual) often signals big news or strong interest, meaning lots of people are suddenly reacting to something.', 15),
  ('2026-08-21', 'When you want to buy a stock, the ''ask'' price is the amount you''ll pay, and the ''bid'' is what a buyer will pay you to sell. What is the ''spread''?', '["The difference between the bid and ask prices","The fee your bank charges to hold the stock","The total number of shares available to trade"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The spread is simply the gap between the bid (what buyers offer) and the ask (what sellers want). A smaller spread usually means the stock is easy to trade, while a wide spread can cost you more when buying and selling.', 15),
  ('2026-08-22', 'If lots of people suddenly want to buy a company''s stock but very few want to sell it, what usually happens to the price?', '["The price goes up","The price goes down","The price stays exactly the same"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When demand (buyers) is higher than supply (sellers), buyers compete and bid the price higher — just like a popular item at an auction.', 15),
  ('2026-08-23', 'A company reports strong earnings, but its stock barely moves. Why might this happen?', '["The good news was already ''priced in''","The company lied about its earnings","Stocks never react to earnings news","The stock market was closed that day"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When investors expect good news, they buy the stock ahead of time, so the price already reflects it. This is called being ''priced in,'' meaning the actual announcement causes little movement.', 15),
  ('2026-08-24', 'On a candlestick chart, the top and bottom tips of the thin lines sticking out (called the ''wicks'') show what?', '["The opening and closing prices","The highest and lowest prices during that time period","The average price for the day"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The thin wicks reach up to the highest price and down to the lowest price reached during that period, while the thicker body shows where the price opened and closed.', 15),
  ('2026-08-25', 'You look at a stock''s 1-day chart and see it dropped sharply, but the 1-year chart shows it climbing steadily. What''s the main difference between these two views?', '["The 1-day chart shows today''s ups and downs, while the 1-year chart shows the bigger, long-term trend","The 1-day chart is always more accurate than the 1-year chart","The 1-year chart only shows made-up numbers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A 1-day chart zooms in on short-term wiggles that happen minute by minute, while a 1-year chart zooms out to show the overall direction over time. A stock can dip for a day but still be trending up for the year.', 15),
  ('2026-08-26', 'On a stock chart, what does a simple moving average (SMA) line show you?', '["The exact price the stock will hit tomorrow","The average price over a set number of past days, updated as time passes","The total number of shares a company has issued","The company''s profit for the year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An SMA smooths out daily ups and downs by averaging the closing prices over a chosen period (like 50 days), helping you see the overall trend rather than the noisy day-to-day jumps.', 15),
  ('2026-08-27', 'Why does spreading your money across many different investments help lower your risk?', '["Because if one investment drops, others may hold steady or rise, softening the blow","Because owning more investments guarantees you can''t lose money","Because the government protects investors who own many stocks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Diversification means not putting all your eggs in one basket. When your money is spread out, a loss in one investment can be balanced by gains or stability in others, so no single bad event wrecks your whole portfolio.', 15),
  ('2026-08-28', 'What''s the main difference between buying a single stock and buying an ETF?', '["A single stock is one company, while an ETF holds many companies in one purchase","A single stock is always cheaper than an ETF","An ETF lets you own only one company at a time","There is no real difference between them"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A single stock is a share of just one company, so your money rides on that one business. An ETF (exchange-traded fund) bundles many companies together, so one purchase spreads your money across lots of them, lowering the risk of any single company hurting you.', 15),
  ('2026-08-29', 'What does "dollar-cost averaging" mean when investing?', '["Investing a fixed amount of money at regular times, no matter the price","Only buying stocks when prices are at their lowest","Putting all your money in at once to save on fees","Averaging the prices of every stock you own"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dollar-cost averaging means investing the same amount on a regular schedule (like $100 every month), which smooths out your buying price over time and takes the guesswork out of timing the market.', 15),
  ('2026-08-30', 'When you buy a share of stock, what do you actually own?', '["A loan the company must pay back to you","A tiny piece of ownership in the company","A guarantee that you''ll make money","A discount on the company''s products"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A share represents a small slice of ownership in a company, so as a shareholder you own a piece of the business and can benefit if it grows in value.', 15),
  ('2026-08-31', 'What''s the basic difference between owning a stock and owning a bond?', '["A stock makes you a part-owner of a company, while a bond means you loaned the company money","A stock is a loan to the company, while a bond makes you an owner","There is no difference; they are two words for the same thing","A stock is only for banks, while a bond is only for governments"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When you buy a stock, you own a small slice of the company and share in its success. When you buy a bond, you''re lending money to a company or government that agrees to pay you back with interest.', 15),
  ('2026-09-01', 'You want to buy a share of a company. What does a stock exchange actually do?', '["It sets the company''s business goals and hires its workers","It connects buyers and sellers and matches them when their prices agree","It lends you the money to buy the shares","It guarantees that every stock will go up in value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A stock exchange is like a giant marketplace: it matches someone wanting to buy at a certain price with someone wanting to sell at that price, and the trade happens when the two prices meet.', 15),
  ('2026-09-02', 'You look up a stock and see: Last price $50, Change -$2, % Change -4%. What does this tell you?', '["The stock costs $52 now and went up by $2","The stock costs $50 now and dropped $2 (4%) from yesterday","The stock will drop another 4% tomorrow","The stock costs $48 now"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ''last price'' is the most recent trade price ($50), and the change shows it fell $2 from the prior day''s close — which is a 4% drop since $2 is 4% of the earlier $52 price.', 15),
  ('2026-09-03', 'What does a company''s "market cap" tell you, and how do you calculate it?', '["The company''s total profit for the year","The share price multiplied by the total number of shares","The amount of cash the company has in the bank","The price of a single share of the company"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Market cap (short for market capitalization) is the total value of a company, found by multiplying the price of one share by how many shares exist. It helps you see how big a company is, not just what one share costs.', 15),
  ('2026-09-04', 'When you see a stock''s ''52-week high'' and ''52-week low,'' what are they telling you?', '["The highest and lowest prices the stock traded at over the past year","How much profit the company made last year","The price you''re guaranteed to pay if you buy today","The number of shares the company has sold in 52 weeks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 52-week high and low simply show the top and bottom prices a stock reached during the past year, giving you a quick sense of its trading range and how today''s price compares.', 15),
  ('2026-09-05', 'When you hear that a stock had a ''volume spike,'' what does that mean?', '["The stock''s price suddenly went up","Way more shares than usual were bought and sold that day","The company paid a bigger dividend to owners","The stock split into more shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume is simply the number of shares traded in a period. A volume spike means unusually heavy buying and selling, which often signals that big news or strong interest is moving the stock.', 15),
  ('2026-09-06', 'When you want to buy a stock, the ''ask'' price is the amount sellers want, and the ''bid'' is what buyers offer. What is the gap between these two prices called?', '["The commission","The spread","The dividend","The premium"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The spread is the difference between the bid (what buyers offer) and the ask (what sellers want). A smaller spread usually means the stock is easy to trade, while a large spread can cost you a bit more when buying and selling.', 15),
  ('2026-09-07', 'Why does a stock''s price usually go up on a given day?', '["More people want to buy it than sell it","The company mails out extra shares","A government office sets the new price each morning","The stock gets tired of being cheap"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Stock prices move based on supply and demand: when more buyers want in than sellers want out, buyers compete and push the price up. News or events (called catalysts) often spark that extra demand.', 15),
  ('2026-09-08', 'A company reports strong earnings, exactly as everyone predicted. Its stock barely moves. Why?', '["The good news was already ''priced in''","The stock market was closed that day","The company made a mistake in its report","Investors didn''t hear the news"]'::jsonb, 0, '''Priced in'' means investors already expected the good news and had bought the stock ahead of time, so the price didn''t change once the news actually arrived.', 15),
  ('2026-09-09', 'On a candlestick chart, the top of the tall thin line (called a ''wick'') sticking out above the main body shows what?', '["The highest price the stock reached that day","The price where trading started that day","The lowest price the stock reached that day"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The thin wick above the body marks the ''high'' — the highest price reached during that period. The opening and closing prices form the top and bottom edges of the thicker body instead.', 15),
  ('2026-09-10', 'You want to see if a stock has generally grown over a long time, not just how it moved today. Which chart should you look at?', '["The 1-day chart","The 1-year chart","Neither one shows price"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A 1-year chart shows the bigger, long-term trend, while a 1-day chart only shows tiny ups and downs from a single day that don''t tell you much about growth over time.', 15),
  ('2026-09-11', 'On a stock chart, what does a simple moving average (SMA) line show you?', '["The exact price the stock will hit tomorrow","The average price over a set number of past days, smoothed into a line","The total number of shares a company has issued","The company''s yearly profit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An SMA adds up a stock''s closing prices over a chosen period (like 50 days) and averages them, creating a smooth line that helps you see the overall price trend without the daily ups and downs.', 15),
  ('2026-09-12', 'Why does spreading your money across many different investments help lower your risk?', '["Because if one investment drops, others may hold steady or rise, softening the blow","Because it guarantees you will never lose any money","Because owning more investments always means bigger profits","Because it lets you avoid paying any taxes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Diversification means not putting all your eggs in one basket. When your money is spread out, a loss in one investment can be balanced by others doing okay, so your whole portfolio doesn''t rise or fall on a single bet.', 15),
  ('2026-09-13', 'What''s the main difference between buying a single stock and buying an ETF?', '["A single stock is one company, while an ETF is a bundle of many companies in one purchase","A single stock always makes money, while an ETF always loses money","There is no difference between them","An ETF is only for rich people, while a single stock is for everyone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A single stock ties your money to just one company, but an ETF (exchange-traded fund) holds many stocks at once, so buying one spreads your money across lots of companies automatically.', 15),
  ('2026-09-14', 'What does "dollar-cost averaging" mean?', '["Investing a fixed amount of money on a regular schedule, no matter the price","Waiting until prices drop before investing all your money at once","Only buying investments when they are cheapest"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dollar-cost averaging means putting in the same dollar amount at regular times (like $100 every month), so you buy more shares when prices are low and fewer when high—removing the stress of trying to time the market.', 15),
  ('2026-09-15', 'When you buy a share of stock, what are you actually getting?', '["A loan you gave the company that they must pay back","A tiny piece of ownership in the company","A guarantee that your money will grow every year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A share represents a small slice of ownership in a company, so as a shareholder you own a part of the business and can benefit if it grows in value.', 15),
  ('2026-09-16', 'You buy a stock and a bond from the same company. What''s the basic difference between them?', '["A stock makes you a part-owner of the company, while a bond means you loaned the company money","A stock means you loaned money, while a bond makes you a part-owner","They are exactly the same thing with different names","Both make you a full owner of the company"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A stock is a small slice of ownership, so you share in the company''s ups and downs. A bond is a loan you give the company, and they promise to pay you back with interest.', 15),
  ('2026-09-17', 'When you buy a stock, what does a stock exchange actually do?', '["It sets the price you must pay for the stock","It matches your buy order with someone else''s sell order","It lends you the money to buy the stock","It stores your shares in a physical safe"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A stock exchange is like a giant marketplace: it connects buyers and sellers and matches your buy order with someone willing to sell at a price you both accept, so the trade can happen.', 15),
  ('2026-09-18', 'You look up a stock and see: Last price $52, Change -$1.30, % Change -2.5%. What does this tell you?', '["The stock costs $1.30 to buy","The stock is up 2.5% and now costs more","The stock fell $1.30, which is about 2.5% lower than its previous price","The stock will drop 2.5% tomorrow"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The ''last price'' is the most recent trade price ($52), and the change shows it dropped $1.30 from the prior close — a fall of about 2.5%. The minus sign means down, not up, and it describes what already happened, not a prediction.', 15),
  ('2026-09-19', 'What does a company''s ''market cap'' tell you, and how is it calculated?', '["The total value of all its shares, found by multiplying the share price by the number of shares","The amount of cash the company has in the bank right now","How much profit the company made last year","The price of buying just one share"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Market cap (short for market capitalization) is the total value of a company''s shares, calculated by multiplying the current share price by the total number of shares. It helps you gauge a company''s overall size rather than just the price of one share.', 15),
  ('2026-09-20', 'On a stock page, you see a ''52-week high'' of $80 and a ''52-week low'' of $40. What do these two numbers tell you?', '["The highest and lowest prices the stock reached over the past year","The most the company earned and lost last year","The price the stock will hit next year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 52-week high and low show the top and bottom prices a stock traded at over the past year, giving you a quick sense of its recent price range — not its profits or any future prediction.', 15),
  ('2026-09-21', 'When traders talk about a stock''s ''volume,'' what do they mean?', '["The price of one share of the stock","The number of shares bought and sold during a period","The total money a company earns in a year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume is simply how many shares changed hands in a given time, like a day. A sudden ''volume spike'' — way more trading than usual — signals that something newsworthy may be happening and lots of people are reacting.', 15),
  ('2026-09-22', 'When you want to buy a stock right away, which price do you usually pay?', '["The bid price (what buyers offer)","The ask price (what sellers want)","A price exactly in the middle","Whatever price you type in"]'::jsonb, 1, 'To buy immediately, you pay the ''ask'' — the lowest price a seller is willing to accept. The ''bid'' is what buyers offer, and the gap between them is called the spread.', 15),
  ('2026-09-23', 'Why does a company''s stock price usually go up?', '["The company prints more shares to sell","More people want to buy the stock than sell it","The government sets a higher price each morning","The stock gets older and more valuable"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stock prices move based on supply and demand: when more people want to buy than sell, buyers compete and push the price up. News like strong earnings (a ''catalyst'') can spark that extra demand.', 15),
  ('2026-09-24', 'A company reports strong earnings that everyone already expected, yet its stock barely moves. Why?', '["The good news was already ''priced in'' before the report","The earnings report must have been fake","Stocks never move on earnings news","The company hid bad news in the report"]'::jsonb, 0, '''Priced in'' means investors already expected the good news and bought the stock beforehand, so the actual announcement brings no surprise and little price change.', 15),
  ('2026-09-25', 'On a single candlestick, what does the ''high'' represent?', '["The price when trading started for that time period","The highest price reached during that time period","The price when trading ended for that time period","The average price for that time period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ''high'' is the highest price the investment reached during that period, shown by the top of the thin line (the wick) sticking out of the candle body. The open and close are where trading started and ended, marking the top and bottom of the thicker body.', 15),
  ('2026-09-26', 'You look at a stock''s 1-day chart and see it dropped 3%, but the 1-year chart shows it''s up 40%. What''s the smarter takeaway?', '["The stock is doing badly and you should worry","The stock has grown a lot over time, despite a small dip today","The two charts must have an error","Only today''s chart matters for long-term investors"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A 1-day chart shows tiny short-term wiggles, while a 1-year chart shows the bigger trend. Zooming out helps you avoid panicking over normal daily ups and downs.', 15),
  ('2026-09-27', 'On a stock chart, what does a simple moving average (SMA) line show you?', '["The average price over a set number of past days, smoothed into one line","The exact price the stock will hit tomorrow","The total number of shares a company has issued","The company''s yearly profit"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A simple moving average adds up a stock''s closing prices over a set period (like 50 days) and divides by that number, smoothing out day-to-day bumps so you can see the general trend more clearly.', 15),
  ('2026-09-28', 'Why does spreading your money across many different investments lower your risk?', '["Because owning more investments guarantees you''ll make money","Because if one investment drops, others may hold steady or rise, softening the blow","Because it lets you avoid paying any taxes","Because more investments always grow faster than fewer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Diversification means not putting all your eggs in one basket. When your money is spread out, a single bad performer has less impact because other investments can balance out the loss.', 15),
  ('2026-09-29', 'What''s the main difference between buying a single stock and buying an ETF?', '["A single stock is one company, while an ETF holds many companies in one purchase","A single stock is always cheaper than an ETF","An ETF only holds one company, while a stock holds many","There is no real difference between them"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A single stock is a slice of just one company, so your money rises or falls with that one business. An ETF (exchange-traded fund) bundles many companies together, spreading your risk across all of them in a single purchase.', 15),
  ('2026-09-30', 'What does "dollar-cost averaging" mean when investing?', '["Buying only when prices are at their lowest point","Investing the same fixed amount of money at regular intervals, no matter the price","Spending exactly one dollar every time you invest","Selling your investments as soon as they drop in value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dollar-cost averaging means putting in a set amount (like $100 every month) on a schedule, so you buy more shares when prices are low and fewer when they''re high—which smooths out the ups and downs over time.', 15),
  ('2026-10-01', 'When you buy a share of stock, what are you actually getting?', '["A loan you gave to the company that they must pay back","A tiny piece of ownership in the company","A guarantee that you''ll make money","A discount coupon for the company''s products"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A share of stock represents partial ownership in a company, so owning even one share makes you a small part-owner and gives you a stake in how the business does.', 15),
  ('2026-10-02', 'What''s the basic difference between owning a stock and owning a bond?', '["A stock means you own a piece of a company; a bond means you lent money to be paid back with interest","A stock is always safer than a bond","A bond means you own part of the company; a stock is a loan you make","There is no real difference between them"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A stock makes you a part-owner of a company, so you share in its success or failure. A bond is like an IOU: you lend money and expect it back later plus interest.', 15),
  ('2026-10-03', 'When you buy a stock, what does a stock exchange actually do?', '["It sets the price of the stock by itself","It matches your buy order with someone else''s sell order","It lends you the money to buy the stock","It guarantees the stock will go up in value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A stock exchange is like a big marketplace: it connects buyers and sellers and matches your buy order with someone willing to sell at that price. Prices come from what buyers and sellers agree on, not from the exchange itself.', 15),
  ('2026-10-04', 'You look up a stock and see: Last Price $50, Change +$1, % Change +2%. What does the ''+$1'' tell you?', '["The stock is worth $1 total","The price went up $1 from where it started the day","You owe $1 to buy it","The stock will go up $1 tomorrow"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ''Change'' shows how much the price moved from the previous day''s closing price, so +$1 means the stock is $1 higher than yesterday''s close, and +2% expresses that same $1 move as a percentage of the price.', 15),
  ('2026-10-05', 'What does a company''s "market cap" tell you, and how is it calculated?', '["The total value of all its shares, found by multiplying share price by the number of shares","The price of just one share of its stock","The amount of money the company keeps in the bank","The profit the company made last year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Market cap (short for market capitalization) is the total value of a company''s shares. You calculate it by multiplying the current share price by the number of shares that exist, which helps you judge a company''s overall size.', 15),
  ('2026-10-06', 'What do a stock''s 52-week high and low tell you?', '["The highest and lowest prices the stock traded at over the past year","The most the company earned and lost in profits last year","A guarantee of where the stock price will go next year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 52-week high and low simply show the top and bottom prices a stock reached during the past year, giving you a quick sense of its recent price range. They describe past prices only and don''t predict future movement.', 15),
  ('2026-10-07', 'You notice a stock suddenly has way more shares being bought and sold than usual today. What does this ''volume spike'' usually signal?', '["The stock is guaranteed to go up soon","Something important is happening that has investors'' attention","The company is about to go out of business","Nothing at all — volume never means anything"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume is simply how many shares change hands in a period. A sudden spike means lots of people are trading, often because of big news or an event — but it doesn''t tell you which direction the price will go.', 15),
  ('2026-10-08', 'When you want to buy a stock, the price you pay is the ''ask,'' and when you sell, you get the ''bid.'' What is the gap between these two prices called?', '["The dividend","The spread","The commission","The margin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The spread is the difference between the bid (what buyers offer) and the ask (what sellers want). A smaller spread usually means the stock trades easily, while a larger spread can cost you more when buying and selling.', 15),
  ('2026-10-09', 'Imagine a company just announced it earned way more money than people expected. What usually happens to its stock price?', '["It tends to go up, because more people want to buy it","It always stays exactly the same","It tends to go down, because good news scares investors"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Good news like strong earnings is a ''catalyst'' that makes more people want to buy the stock. When demand (buyers) rises faster than supply (sellers), the price tends to go up.', 15),
  ('2026-10-10', 'A company reports strong earnings that everyone expected, yet its stock barely moves. Why might this happen?', '["The news was already ''priced in'' before the report","The good news is bad luck for investors","Earnings never affect stock prices","The stock market was closed that day"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When investors expect good news, they often buy the stock ahead of time, pushing the price up early. By the time the news arrives, it''s already ''priced in,'' so the actual announcement causes little movement.', 15)
